#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91f5bc72-7a18-54b9-a102-577832861b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325445} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f99572-65b3-5bf9-9c0f-a70538c178a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={cos}^{2}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325446} {:answer/id #uuid "607710bd-30b2-519e-b72c-a742e6794283" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325447} {:answer/id #uuid "78121f4f-47a1-541f-972f-e9d12f52e9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325448} {:answer/id #uuid "a187b14f-380c-54f2-8562-6442ae532af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f5bc72-7a18-54b9-a102-577832861b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325445} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6fad4bb-6159-5bc5-8de7-a37512e9b550" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66e6fd67-7c2f-53c8-9b0c-0cf636baf830" :question/math-academy-id "q-122393" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}={sin}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3.$"}]}
